I've written two task files for this session and nothing is implemented yet. The commit step didn't go through: session_plan/ is gitignored, so git add refused it and nothing was committed. The files exist on disk (task_01.md, task_02.md, issue_responses.md), and the harness command ends in || true, so I left it there.

Task 1 — Part of #1026 (product). This carries out @yuanhao's 2026-11-03 decision on exit codes for yoyo -p:
• Cut off by a limit the user never set (a preset or the base default): exit 1.
• Cut off by `max_tokens` the user set (flag or .yoyo.toml): exit 0 with the existing stderr note.
• Refused by the model: exit 1.

The task description points to where the work goes:
• Who set the limit: the existing AgentConfig.max_tokens: Option<u32> already holds the answer, so nothing new has to be built.
• The rule: a pure function in output_limit.rs.
• Getting it out: one new field on PromptOutcome, read by exit_code_for_outcome.

The task's first test is the real binary against a stub server for all three cases, plus a deliberate break that has to make the tests fail and is then undone. It also has to check which of the repo's scripts and workflows call yoyo -p. This repo sets max_tokens, so its own runs stay at exit 0 on a cut. A refusal in its own runs will now exit 1, and the commit has to say what each call site does with that. The json/stream-json note, the other half of the decision, stays open on #1026.

Task 2 — Fixes #1119 (product). This closes one issue from the backlog, which has 14 open. The xai, mistral and zai defaults (grok-4, mistral-large-latest, glm-4-plus) either move to the models yoagent has presets for (grok-4.7, mistral-large-4, glm-5.3) or keep their current id with a reason written beside it. Either way the reason covers price and capability, and says that existing users without a configured model would silently move. One new test then checks every provider's default: it must either have a preset or be listed with a stated reason. The same task rewrites the OpenRouter default's outdated reason. The real reason now is price: $2/$10 per million tokens against $3/$15 for the older Sonnet 4.6.

